{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}Our crawler · Boulangerie{% endblock %}
{% block description %}What RoboCroissantBot fetches from your site, and how to let it in.{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article class="prose crawler">
  <h1>Our crawler, and how to let it in</h1>
  <p class="lede">To write your croissant, we read your public download pages and the files they list. Some sites
    block automated visitors, through <code>robots.txt</code> or a service like Cloudflare. That's your choice, and
    we respect it. If you'd like your croissant to be complete, here's how to let our crawler in, and only ours.</p>

  <h2>Who we are</h2>
  <p>Our crawler identifies itself in every request with this user agent:</p>
  <pre><code>{{ user_agent }}</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>What it fetches:</strong> your download pages, the files they list, and the pages that describe your
      resource (about, license, how to cite).</li>
    <li><strong>How often:</strong> a full read when we rebuild croissants (about once a month), and a weekly check of
      a small sample of download links (at most {{ link_sample }} per resource).</li>
    <li><strong>How fast:</strong> at most one request per second to your site, slower if your
      <code>robots.txt</code> sets a <code>Crawl-delay</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Who runs it:</strong> the Renaissance Computing Institute (RENCI) at UNC-Chapel Hill.
      <a href="https://github.com/{{ repo }}/issues/new?template=crawler-access.yml">Contact us</a> any time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Ways to let us in</h2>
  <p>Pick whichever suits your site. Then <a href="https://github.com/{{ repo }}/issues/new?template=crawler-access.yml">tell
    us which you chose</a>, so we can start using it.</p>

  <h3>1. Allow us in <code>robots.txt</code></h3>
  <p>If your site blocks crawlers through <code>robots.txt</code>, add a section for ours. Everyone else stays
    blocked. Limit it to the parts of your site we need:</p>
  <pre><code>User-agent: RoboCroissantBot
Allow: /downloads/
Allow: /about/
Crawl-delay: 2</code></pre>

  <h3>2. Let us through your firewall (Cloudflare and similar)</h3>
  <p>A <code>robots.txt</code> rule doesn't get us past a firewall or a "checking your browser" page. Your site's
    administrator can add an exception for our requests. Because anyone can copy a user agent, an exception should
    also check one of these:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Our addresses.</strong> We crawl from a fixed set of IP addresses at RENCI. Ask us for the current
      list.</li>
    <li><strong>A secret header.</strong> You choose a value, and we send it with every request as
      <code>X-Croissant-Access</code>. Tell us you've set one up, and we'll arrange to get the value from you
      privately. Please <strong>never post it in a GitHub issue</strong>, since issues are public.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In Cloudflare, this is a custom rule with the action "Skip", matching our user agent and our addresses or
    header. Some settings, such as Bot Fight Mode on the free plan, can't make exceptions. If that's what's
    blocking us, option 3 may be easier.</p>

  <h3>3. Publish a list of your files</h3>
  <p>Often the simplest. Put a list of your downloadable files at a fixed address: a sitemap, a JSON or text
    file with one link per line, or a page listing them all. Then we read that one file instead of crawling, and we
    only need to be able to download the files it lists.</p>

  <h2>To block us</h2>
  <p>Add this to your <code>robots.txt</code>, and we'll stop:</p>
  <pre><code>User-agent: RoboCroissantBot
Disallow: /</code></pre>
</article>
{% endblock %}
